window.CANOPY_SEARCH = [{"url": "wiki/index.html", "title": "The Canopy wiki", "section": "Start here", "lede": "Guides for learning git with Canopy, recipes for everyday fixes, and a complete reference. New to git? Start with Git in ten minutes.", "headings": [{"id": "guides", "text": "Guides"}, {"id": "recipes", "text": "Recipes"}, {"id": "reference", "text": "Reference"}], "text": "New here? Read Git in ten minutes , then Your daily workflow . Already comfortable with git? Jump to the recipes, or look up a key on the Keys page. Guides  Git in ten minutes Repositories, the staging area, commits, branches and remotes, each shown where it lives in Canopy. Your daily workflow Edit, stage, commit, push, open a PR: the loop you'll do every day. Branches, merging and rebasing Why branches, how merging and rebasing differ, and what to do when they collide. Working with GitHub Connect GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI from your terminal. Recipes  Fix your last commit Typo in the message, forgot a file, or want to fold a fix into an older commit. Undo a mistake Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back \"lost\" work. Resolve a merge conflict Resolve conflicts one at a time, then finish the merge or rebase. Tidy up commits before sharing Squash, reorder, reword or drop commits with a visual interactive rebase. Find the commit that broke something Bisect to the breaking commit, then blame and file history to understand it. Work on two branches at once Check out a second branch in its own folder with worktrees. Review a pull request Check out a PR, read the diff, comment on lines, then approve or request changes. Fix a failing CI run Find why CI failed in the full log, fix it, push, and re-run. Reference  Configuration Every setting in config.toml, with its default. Command line Flags, arguments and environment variables. Themes The five built-in themes and their colors. Troubleshooting Fixes for the problems people actually hit. FAQ Short answers to common questions."}, {"url": "wiki/git-basics.html", "title": "Git in ten minutes", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The handful of ideas that make git make sense, and where to see each one in Canopy.", "headings": [{"id": "repo", "text": "A repository is a project with a memory"}, {"id": "three-places", "text": "Your changes live in three places"}, {"id": "commits", "text": "A commit is a saved snapshot"}, {"id": "branches", "text": "A branch is a line of work"}, {"id": "remotes", "text": "A remote is the same repo somewhere else"}, {"id": "safety", "text": "You can almost always undo"}], "text": "A repository is a project with a memory  A repository (repo) is a folder whose history git tracks. Everything git knows lives in a hidden .git folder inside it. Run canopy in any repo and the Home tab ( 1 ) shows where things stand. canopy — Home Your changes live in three places  The working tree is the files you edit. Changes here are listed under Changes on the Changes tab ( 2 ). The staging area (also called the index) is where you gather what goes into the next commit. Staged files appear under Staged . History is the commits themselves, on the History tab ( 3 ). Staging is what lets you commit part of your work. In Canopy you can stage a whole file with space , or step into its diff with ↵ and stage single lines. canopy — Changes A commit is a saved snapshot  A commit records the staged changes with a message saying why. Each commit points to the one before it, which is how history forms a chain. Press c , write a short summary (Canopy counts to 50 characters for you), and press ↵ . Good messages say why : \"Fix crash when the repo has no commits\" beats \"update main.rs\". A branch is a line of work  A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, your current branch moves forward. Branches let you try something without disturbing main . The Branches tab ( 4 ) lists them; n makes a new one and space switches to it. HEAD means \"where you are now\"; History shows it as HEAD → main . A remote is the same repo somewhere else  A remote (usually called origin ) is a copy of the repo on a server such as GitHub. Push ( P ) sends your commits there; pull ( p ) brings others' commits to you; fetch ( f ) checks what's new without changing your files. The header shows ↑2 ↓1 when you're 2 commits ahead and 1 behind. You can almost always undo  Git rarely forgets anything. z undoes your last commit, reset, merge or checkout, and the Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere you've been so you can go back further. See Undo a mistake . Learn as you go: keep teach mode on (it is by default). The footer shows the git command behind every action you take, so the ideas on this page turn into commands you'll recognize."}, {"url": "wiki/daily-workflow.html", "title": "Your daily workflow", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The loop you'll repeat every day: pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a pull request.", "headings": [{"id": "tips", "text": "Tips"}], "text": "Start up to date. Open Canopy in your repo and press p to pull. Home tells you if you're behind. Make a branch for your change. Go to Branches ( 4 ), press n , and name it after the change, like fix-login-timeout . Canopy switches to it. Edit your files in your editor as usual. Canopy notices changes within a few seconds; ⌃R refreshes right away. Review and stage. On Changes ( 2 ), read each file's diff on the right. Press space to stage a file, or ↵ to go into the diff and stage only some lines. Commit. Press c , write a summary, press ↵ . Repeat steps 3–5 as you go: small commits are easier to review and to undo. Push. Press P . The first push of a new branch also sets it up to track the remote. Open a pull request. Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press n . Give it a title and a description of what changed and why. Watch CI. Actions ( 0 ) shows your branch's runs updating live. Home's GitHub card also shows whether checks pass. Tips  Not ready to commit but need to switch tasks? S stashes your changes; pop them back from the Stash tab ( 5 ). Committed too early? On Changes, A amends the last commit. See Fix your last commit . Forgot where you were? Home's Next steps says what to do next."}, {"url": "wiki/branches.html", "title": "Branches, merging and rebasing", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Why branches matter, the two ways to combine them, and what to do when they collide.", "headings": [{"id": "why", "text": "Why branch"}, {"id": "merge", "text": "Merging"}, {"id": "rebase", "text": "Rebasing"}, {"id": "conflicts", "text": "When they collide"}, {"id": "tracking", "text": "Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\""}], "text": "Why branch  A branch keeps a change separate until it's ready. main stays working while you experiment, and you can drop a branch without affecting anything else. Branches are cheap: make one for every change. Merging  Merging brings another branch's commits into yours. If both branches moved on, git makes a merge commit with two parents; History draws it as ○ where two lines join. On Branches ( 4 ), select the branch to bring in and press M . Rebasing  Rebasing replays your commits on top of another branch, as if you'd started from its latest commit. History stays a straight line, with no merge commit. Select the branch to rebase onto and press r . Which should I use? Merge when a branch's history matters as its own story, or when others already have your commits. Rebase to keep your own unshared work tidy before opening a PR. Never rebase commits other people have pulled: rebasing rewrites them. When they collide  If both branches changed the same lines, git stops and asks you to choose. The header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE ), and Home says what to do. Follow Resolve a merge conflict . Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\"  A branch's upstream is the remote branch it syncs with, like origin/main . The Branches tab shows ↑1 ↓2 (1 commit to push, 2 to pull) or ✓ when in sync. u sets the upstream if it's missing; pushing a new branch with P sets it for you."}, {"url": "wiki/github-flow.html", "title": "Working with GitHub", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Connect Canopy to GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI without leaving the terminal.", "headings": [{"id": "connect", "text": "Connect GitHub"}, {"id": "prs", "text": "Pull requests (8)"}, {"id": "issues", "text": "Issues (9)"}, {"id": "notifications", "text": "Notifications"}, {"id": "actions", "text": "Actions (0)"}, {"id": "releases", "text": "Releases"}], "text": "Connect GitHub  Canopy uses the official GitHub CLI, gh , with your own login. Install and log in once: $ brew install gh $ gh auth login Canopy never installs anything for you. If gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs say exactly what to run. Pull requests (8)  See every open PR with its checks and review state. f cycles open, yours, review requested and all. The side panel shows the description, checks, reviews and comments; D adds the diff. canopy — Pull requests Issues (9)  Browse issues with labels and comments. n files a new one, C comments, X closes (or reopens). Notifications  Press ] on Issues for your GitHub inbox: review requests, mentions and CI results. ↵ opens one in the browser, m marks it read, M marks everything read. f switches between this repository and everywhere, u between unread and all. Actions (0)  Runs for your branch, updating live. Select a failed run to see the failing step and the end of its log, then R to re-run it. For more, press L to open the whole log. Failed jobs come first and the viewer starts on the first error. / searches and n / N jump between matches. Releases  Press ] on Actions to see releases with their notes and downloads. n creates one. Canopy suggests the next tag, and GitHub writes the notes if you leave them empty. For the full key list, see the GitHub page ."}, {"url": "wiki/fix-last-commit.html", "title": "Fix your last commit", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "A typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix that belongs in an older commit.", "headings": [{"id": "message", "text": "Change the last commit's message"}, {"id": "forgot", "text": "Add a forgotten file"}, {"id": "older", "text": "Fold a fix into an older commit"}], "text": "Change the last commit's message  On Changes ( 2 ), press A (amend). The dialog opens with the current message. Edit it and press ↵ . Add a forgotten file  On Changes ( 2 ), stage the file with space . Press A , keep the message, press ↵ . The file is now part of the last commit. Fold a fix into an older commit  Stage the fix. On History ( 3 ), select the commit it belongs to and press F . Canopy makes a fixup! commit. To squash it in, press i on the commit before it and start the rebase; see Tidy up commits . Already pushed? Amending rewrites the commit, so the next push needs force. Canopy offers \"force push (with lease)\", which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime. Only do this on branches nobody else is using."}, {"url": "wiki/undo.html", "title": "Undo a mistake", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back work you thought was lost.", "headings": [{"id": "z", "text": "The quick way: z"}, {"id": "further", "text": "Going further back: the reflog"}, {"id": "discard", "text": "Throw away uncommitted edits"}], "text": "The quick way: z  z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, and tells you exactly what it will do before it does it: After a commit: the commit is undone and its changes come back staged , ready to fix and commit again. After a checkout: you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase: the branch moves back and your uncommitted changes are kept. Going further back: the reflog  The Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits no branch points to any more. To get back to any of them: Open Reflog ( 7 ) and find the entry from before the mistake. The side panel shows that commit. Press r and choose Mixed to move your branch there and keep your files as they are, or Hard to make your files match it too. Throw away uncommitted edits  On Changes, select the file and press d . Canopy tells you exactly what will be lost and asks first. This one can't be undone, because git never saved those edits."}, {"url": "wiki/conflicts.html", "title": "Resolve a merge conflict", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "When a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stops because both sides changed the same lines.", "headings": [], "text": "You'll know you have a conflict because the header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE or CHERRY-PICK ) and Home says how many files are conflicted. Go to Changes ( 2 ). Conflicted files are listed first, under Conflicts . Select one and press ↵ . The panel shows each conflict with ours (your side) and theirs (the side you're bringing in). For each conflict, press o to keep yours, t to take theirs, or b to keep both. j / k move between conflicts. Canopy writes the file as you go. When a file has no conflicts left, Canopy stages it for you. When all files are resolved, press C to continue the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts Need something in between? Press e to open the file in your editor, fix it by hand, save, and come back. Changed your mind about a choice? u puts the conflict markers back. Want out entirely? X aborts and returns everything to how it was."}, {"url": "wiki/clean-history.html", "title": "Tidy up commits before sharing", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Squash, reorder and drop commits with a visual interactive rebase.", "headings": [], "text": "Before opening a PR, you might want to turn \"wip\", \"fix typo\" and \"actually fix it\" into one clear commit. That's an interactive rebase . On History ( 3 ), select the oldest commit you want to change and press i . The rebase editor lists those commits, oldest at the top. For each one choose: p pick : keep it as is. s squash : combine with the commit above, keeping both messages. f fixup : combine with the commit above, dropping this message. e edit : stop at this commit so you can change it. d drop : remove it. Move commits up and down with K and J . Press ↵ to run it. If it stops on a conflict, resolve it as in Resolve a merge conflict and press C . Don't rewrite shared history. Only tidy commits you haven't pushed, or ones on a branch that's only yours. If the rebase goes wrong, z undoes it."}, {"url": "wiki/find-a-bug.html", "title": "Find the commit that broke something", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Bisect to the commit that introduced a bug, then use blame and file history to understand it.", "headings": [{"id": "bisect", "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit"}, {"id": "blame", "text": "Blame: who changed this line, and why"}, {"id": "history", "text": "File history"}], "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit  Commit or stash your changes (bisect checks out other commits). On History ( 3 ), select a commit where things still worked and press b . Your current commit counts as broken. Git checks out a commit halfway between. Test it: build it, run it, whatever shows the bug. Press b and answer good or bad (or skip if you can't test it). The header shows about how many steps are left. When git finds the culprit, choose Finish and show it . Canopy stops bisecting and selects that commit in History. Blame: who changed this line, and why  On Changes, or on a file inside a commit's diff, press B . Each line shows the commit that last changed it. ↵ jumps to that commit; B again shows the file as it was just before that change, so you can keep digging. canopy — Blame File history  Press L on a file to see only the commits that touched it, following renames. esc goes back to the full history."}, {"url": "wiki/two-branches.html", "title": "Work on two branches at once", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Keep a second branch checked out in its own folder with worktrees.", "headings": [], "text": "Switching branches means stashing or committing half-done work. A worktree avoids that: a second folder for the same repository, with a different branch checked out. Go to Branches ( 4 ) and press ] until you reach Worktrees . Press n and type a branch name (existing or new). Canopy creates the folder next to your repo: acme-app + fix/login becomes acme-app-fix-login . Press ↵ to open it in Canopy, or open the folder in your editor. When you're done, select it and press d to remove it. If you deleted the folder by hand, x tidies up."}, {"url": "wiki/review-a-pr.html", "title": "Review a pull request", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Check out a PR, read it, run it, and leave a review.", "headings": [], "text": "Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press f until it shows review requested . Select the PR. The side panel has the description, checks and existing reviews. Press D to read the diff; review comments show under their lines. To comment on a specific line, press ↵ to move into the diff, put the cursor on the line, press C , write, and press ⌃S . Want to run it? Press space to check out its branch. Press r and choose Approve , Comment , or Request changes . Write your review (Markdown works) and press ⌃S . If it's approved and checks pass, M merges it: merge commit, squash, or rebase. canopy — Pull requests"}, {"url": "wiki/fix-ci.html", "title": "Fix a failing CI run", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Find out why CI failed, fix it, and get back to green without opening a browser.", "headings": [], "text": "Wiki / Recipes Fix a failing CI run Find out why CI failed, fix it, and get back to green without opening a browser. Go to Actions ( 0 ). Your branch's runs are listed, newest first. Select the one with ✗ . The side panel shows which job and step failed and the last lines of their log. Often that's enough. If it isn't, press L for the whole log. It opens on the first error, with failed jobs first. Press / and type something like panicked or error , then n to step through the matches. Fix the code, then stage and commit on Changes ( 2 ) and push with P . A new run starts, and Actions updates live while it runs. If the failure was flaky (a timeout or a network blip), don't change anything. Press R on the failed run to re-run just the failed jobs. Want help? Press A on the failed run: Canopy writes a prompt from the failure and opens Claude Code or Codex in a new tab to fix it ( Fix with AI ). Review its change, then commit and push yourself. canopy — CI runs canopy acme-app main ↑1 ▄ ▀▀▀▀ ▄ ▀ ▀▀ ▀ 1 Home 2 Changes 2 3 Log 4 Refs 5 Stash 6 Repos 7 Reflog 8 PRs 2 9 Issues 0 CI ╭ Actions · Releases main · 2 · live ──────────────────────────╮ ╭ CI #18 ──────────────────────────────────────────╮ │ │ │ │ │ ✗ CI feature/parser pull_request 18h │ │ CI #18 · Add CSV parser │ │ · CI typo push 18h │ │ failure · pull_request on feature/parser · 18h │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ✗ check (ubuntu-latest) │ │ │ │ ✓ Checkout │ │ │ │ ✗ Run cargo test │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ── Failed log (last lines) ── │ │ │ │ thread main panicked at src/lib.rs:10 │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ╰───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ $ teach mode: the git command behind each action appears here (T to hide) ] switch view R re-run L log o browser f filter : palette q quit c commit ? help Still running? The full log is only ready once the run finishes. Until then, the side panel shows each job's progress. Previous Review a pull request Next Start a new repository"}, {"url": "wiki/new-repo.html", "title": "Start a new repository", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Turn a folder into a git repository, then connect it to GitHub over HTTPS or SSH.", "headings": [{"id": "terminal", "text": "In the terminal"}, {"id": "desktop", "text": "In the desktop app"}, {"id": "https-or-ssh", "text": "HTTPS or SSH?"}], "text": "Wiki / Recipes Start a new repository Turn a folder into a git repository, then connect it to GitHub over HTTPS or SSH, from the terminal or the desktop app. In the terminal Run canopy with the folder: canopy ~/code/my-app (or just canopy inside it). If there's no repository there, Canopy says so instead of stopping. Press c to create one. Pick a branch name (most projects use main ), a .gitignore for your language, and whether to start with a README (and an MIT license) and a first commit. Canopy runs git init , writes the files, and makes the first commit. Teach mode shows each command. Then connect it to GitHub, or skip and do it later (Branches → Remotes): Create it on GitHub ( g ) if the GitHub CLI is signed in: choose private or public, and Canopy runs gh repo create , adds it as origin and pushes. Connect an existing repository ( e ): make it on github.com first (empty, without a README), then type owner/name or paste its URL, and choose HTTPS or SSH. In the desktop app Choose Start a new one… on the welcome screen, or open any folder that isn't a repository. A three-step dialog asks the same questions, shows the address it will use as you type, and ends with the list of git commands it ran. HTTPS or SSH? Both reach the same repository; they differ in how git proves it's you. HTTPS https://github.com/owner/name.git Works everywhere, even behind strict networks. You sign in through the browser or with a token; gh auth login sets this up for you. The easiest place to start. SSH git@github.com:owner/name.git Uses an SSH key on your computer, so there are no password prompts once the key is added to GitHub. Check it works with ssh -T git@github.com . Canopy suggests the one your GitHub CLI is set to use ( gh config get git_protocol ), or SSH if your computer already has an SSH key. You can switch later: git remote set-url origin <other address> , or edit the remote in Branches → Remotes. Previous Fix a failing CI run Next Configuration"}, {"url": "wiki/config.html", "title": "Configuration", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Every setting in config.toml, with its default.", "headings": [{"id": "keys", "text": "Remapping keys #"}, {"id": "custom", "text": "Custom commands #"}], "text": "The config file is ~/.canopy/config.toml (on Windows, %USERPROFILE%\\.canopy\\config.toml ; set CANOPY_HOME to use another folder). Before 1.0.7 it lived in ~/.config/canopy ; Canopy copies that folder over the first time it runs. canopy --config-path prints where Canopy looks; canopy --example-config prints a starter file. Every setting is optional. theme \"canopy\" Color theme: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light , or the name of your own theme . See Themes . editor unset The editor e opens files in, e.g. \"code --wait\" , \"nvim\" or \"zed --wait\" . Unset means $VISUAL , then $EDITOR , then vi (Notepad on Windows). teach_mode true Show the git command behind each action in the footer. T toggles it while running. confirm_destructive true Ask before discarding changes, hard resets, force deletes and similar. compact false Tighter layout: less padding and no gaps between panels. splash true Play the tree animation when Canopy starts (any key skips it). ai \"auto\" What Fix with AI ( A on a failed run in Actions) opens: auto (the first of Claude Code or Codex that's installed), claude , codex , off , or your own command line, with {prompt} where the prompt goes. ai_open \"tab\" Where it opens: tab (a new tab in tmux, Windows Terminal, iTerm, kitty or WezTerm; a new window otherwise), window , or here (Canopy steps aside until it exits). desktop_tip true About once a week, mention Canopy Desktop when Canopy starts ( ⌥D shows how to get it). Set false to never see it. mac_key_symbols macOS: true , else false Show modifier keys as ⌃ ⌥ ⇧ instead of ctrl- / alt- . nerd_font false Use Nerd Font icons (only turn on if your terminal font has them). workspace_dirs [] Folders the Workspace tab scans for repositories, e.g. [\"~/code\"] . Empty means the folder above the current repo. workspace_depth 3 How many folders deep to look for repositories. log_page_size 300 Commits History loads at a time (more load as you scroll). gh_program unset Path to the GitHub CLI if it isn't gh on your PATH . [keys] empty Remap actions. See below. [[custom_commands]] none Run your own commands from a key. See below. Remapping keys # Map an action's name to a key or a list of keys. Names are in the last column of the Keys page. A key you assign is taken away from whatever used it before, and Canopy warns you if that leaves an action without a key. [keys] toggle_stage = \"s\" commit = [\"c\", \"ctrl-s\"] push = \"alt-p\" Key names: a character ( x , X , ? ), space , enter , esc , tab , backtab , arrows ( up …), pageup , home , f1 – f12 , with ctrl- and/or alt- in front. Custom commands # [[custom_commands]] key = \"X\" cmd = \"git push --force-with-lease\" description = \"force push (safely)\" confirm = true The command runs with sh -c in the repository's root. Its output is shown when it finishes. description is what the command palette and help show; confirm asks first."}, {"url": "wiki/cli.html", "title": "Command line", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Flags, arguments and environment variables.", "headings": [{"id": "env", "text": "Environment variables"}], "text": "$ canopy [OPTIONS] [PATH] PATH Repository to open. Defaults to the current folder. If it isn't a repository yet, Canopy offers to create one . -w , --workspace DIR Start in the Workspace tab, scanning DIR for repositories. -t , --theme THEME Use a theme for this run: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light , or one of yours . --list-themes List the built-in themes and yours, then exit. --export-theme THEME Print a theme as JSON, to start your own from, then exit. --export-settings Print the settings Canopy Desktop shares (theme, editor, AI assistant, teach mode) as JSON, then exit. --import-settings FILE Apply a settings file from --export-settings or Canopy Desktop to your config, then exit. --config-path Print where Canopy reads its config, then exit. --example-config Print a starter config, then exit. -V , --version Print the version. -h , --help Print help. Environment variables # CANOPY_CONFIG Read the config from this file instead of the default location. CANOPY_HOME Use this folder instead of ~/.canopy for the config, themes and Canopy Desktop's settings. CANOPY_GH Path to the GitHub CLI (same as gh_program in the config). VISUAL , EDITOR The editor e opens files in, e.g. code --wait , when editor isn't set in the config (falls back to vi )."}, {"url": "wiki/themes.html", "title": "Themes", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Five built-in themes, plus your own as JSON files (made by hand or in Canopy Desktop's theme editor). Set one with theme = \"…\" in the config, pass -t, or cycle live with ⌃T.", "headings": [{"id": "your-own", "text": "Your own themes"}, {"id": "desktop", "text": "With Canopy Desktop"}, {"id": "sync", "text": "Moving settings between the apps"}], "text": "Your own themes A theme is a small JSON file in ~/.canopy/themes/ (on Windows, %USERPROFILE%\\.canopy\\themes ). Both the terminal app and Canopy Desktop read that folder, so a theme made in one shows up in the other. Start from a built-in one: mkdir -p ~/.canopy/themes canopy --export-theme nord > ~/.canopy/themes/ocean.json Then change its name and any colors you like. Colors you leave out come from base : { \"name\": \"Ocean\", \"base\": \"nord\", \"colors\": { \"bg\": \"#0f1b2a\", \"accent\": \"#4fc1e9\", \"selection_bg\": \"#1d3350\" } } Use it with theme = \"Ocean\" in the config or canopy -t Ocean ; ⌃T cycles through your themes too. canopy --list-themes lists everything Canopy found, and a file with a mistake in it is skipped with a note. Color Used for bg , fg , muted Background, text, and quieter text (times, hints) border , border_focus Panel borders, and the focused panel's border accent , accent_alt Titles and highlights, and a second highlight selection_bg The selected row added , removed , modified , conflict File states and diff lines added_bg , removed_bg Behind added and removed diff lines hash , branch , remote , tag Commit ids, branches, remotes, tags warn , error Warnings and errors With Canopy Desktop Canopy Desktop has a theme editor (Settings, Appearance): pick colors, see them live, and save. Saved themes go in the same folder, so the terminal app can use them straight away. It can also import and export theme files, and open one in your text editor for fine-tuning. Moving settings between the apps The terminal app is set up in config.toml ; the desktop app has its own settings screen. They can swap the settings they share (theme, text editor, AI assistant, showing git commands) as one JSON file: canopy --export-settings > my-canopy.json # theme, editor, ai, teach_mode canopy --import-settings my-canopy.json # updates config.toml (and saves the theme) In Canopy Desktop, Settings has Import from terminal app and Send to terminal app , which do the same through config.toml directly, and Export / Import for the file. Importing only changes those keys; your comments and the rest of the config stay as they are. Themes use 24-bit color. If colors look wrong, your terminal may not support it; see Troubleshooting ."}, {"url": "wiki/troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Fixes for the problems people actually run into.", "headings": [{"id": "not-a-repo", "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\""}, {"id": "gh", "text": "GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in"}, {"id": "option", "text": "Option shortcuts don't work"}, {"id": "glyphs", "text": "Borders or symbols look misaligned"}, {"id": "colors", "text": "Colors look wrong or washed out"}, {"id": "push", "text": "Push was rejected"}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows: \"canopy\" isn't recognized"}, {"id": "windows-display", "text": "Windows: odd characters or colors"}, {"id": "config", "text": "\"Config error, using defaults\""}, {"id": "bug", "text": "Something else"}], "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\"  You passed a folder that isn't a repo. Run canopy inside a repository, or use canopy -w ~/code to pick one from the Workspace tab. GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in  Install the GitHub CLI and log in, then reopen Canopy: brew install gh , gh auth login . If gh lives somewhere unusual, set gh_program in the config. Option shortcuts don't work  Canopy understands both terminals that send Option as Alt/Meta and macOS Terminal's default, where Option types a special character on a US keyboard layout. With other keyboard layouts, turn on Use Option as Meta key (Terminal → Settings → Profiles → Keyboard) or the equivalent in iTerm2 (Profiles → Keys → Left Option key: Esc+). Borders or symbols look misaligned  Canopy only draws characters that are one column wide in standard monospace fonts. If lines still don't meet, your terminal font may be missing box-drawing characters: try SF Mono, Menlo, JetBrains Mono or Fira Code. In iTerm2, turn off Treat ambiguous-width characters as double width . Colors look wrong or washed out  Canopy uses 24-bit (\"true\") color. Some terminals, including older versions of macOS Terminal, don't support it and approximate the colors; iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and Alacritty do support it. Trying another theme with ⌃T can help too. Push was rejected  Someone pushed to the branch before you. Press p to pull first; if your branch has diverged, P offers to pull with rebase, pull with merge, or force push with lease. Windows: \"canopy\" isn't recognized  The folder with canopy.exe isn't on your PATH , or the terminal was opened before you added it. Follow the Windows steps , then open a new terminal. If git isn't found either, install it with winget install --id Git.Git -e . Windows: odd characters or colors  Use Windows Terminal : the old console window (conhost) has limited color and character support. A font like Cascadia Mono (the default) shows every character Canopy uses. \"Config error, using defaults\"  The config file has a typo. The message names the line. canopy --example-config prints a valid starting point. Something else  Open an issue with what you did, what you expected, and your terminal and OS."}, {"url": "wiki/faq.html", "title": "FAQ", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Short answers to common questions.", "headings": [{"id": "which-app", "text": "Which app should I use?"}, {"id": "does-canopy-replace-git", "text": "Does Canopy replace git?"}, {"id": "is-it-safe-to-use-on-important-repositories", "text": "Is it safe to use on important repositories?"}, {"id": "does-it-send-my-code-anywhere", "text": "Does it send my code anywhere?"}, {"id": "does-it-work-on-linux-windows", "text": "Does it work on Linux? Windows?"}, {"id": "can-i-use-it-with-gitlab-or-bitbucket", "text": "Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?"}, {"id": "why-and-but-no-shortcuts", "text": "Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?"}, {"id": "how-do-i-update", "text": "How do I update?"}, {"id": "is-there-a-desktop-app", "text": "Is there a desktop app?"}], "text": "Which app should I use? New to git, or prefer a window? Start with Canopy Desktop. Want everything from the keyboard? Canopy for the terminal is the power tool. Does Canopy replace git?  No. Canopy runs the real git for everything, so your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all work, and you can mix it freely with the command line. Is it safe to use on important repositories?  Canopy asks before anything destructive, and z undoes most actions. Everything it does is a normal git command; turn on teach mode to see each one. Does it send my code anywhere?  No. Git runs locally. GitHub features use your own gh login and only contact GitHub when you open a GitHub tab or act on one. Is there a desktop app? Yes: Canopy Desktop, a release preview for macOS, Windows and Linux. On a Mac: brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop ; on Windows: winget install shankars.canopy-desktop . It isn't notarized by Apple yet, so macOS asks you to confirm the first time you open it. Does it work on Linux? Windows?  Yes, all three. On macOS and Linux install it with Homebrew; on Windows use winget install shankars.canopy , the install script, or the .zip from GitHub Releases (x64 and Arm). See Windows install . Windows Terminal is recommended. Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?  All the git features work with any remote. The GitHub tabs are GitHub-only for now. Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?  Terminals keep the Command key for themselves (copy, paste, new tab) and don't pass it to programs, so Canopy uses Control and Option. How do I update?  brew upgrade canopy , or re-run the cargo install command with --force ."}, {"url": "index.html", "title": "Canopy", "section": "Home", "lede": "Canopy makes git and GitHub easy to see and safe to use: a friendly desktop app to start with, and a terminal app that does everything from the keyboard.", "headings": [], "text": "Everything git does One screen for the whole repository. What changed, what's staged, where your branch stands, and what to do next, in both apps. The ones marked Terminal are in the terminal app, where new power features land first. Stage single lines Open any diff, then stage or unstage a line, a range, or a hunk. Craft commits that say exactly one thing. Undo anything Undo the last commit, reset, merge, or checkout ( ⌘Z in the desktop app, z in the terminal). An undone commit comes back as staged changes. Conflicts, one at a time See ours and theirs for each conflict. Keep one, the other, or both. The file is staged once nothing's left. History that makes sense A commit graph with search in both apps. In the terminal, also file history that follows renames, blame, and a visual interactive rebase. Bisect, guided Terminal Mark a commit that worked, answer good or bad at each step, and Canopy points at the commit that broke things. Every repo, one view Terminal The Workspace tab shows every repository in your code folders: branch, changes, and sync state. GitHub built in Pull requests, reviews, issues, and CI runs, through the official GitHub CLI and your own login. Yours to configure Themes you can make yourself, shared by both apps. In the terminal, also remappable keys, custom commands on any key, and a palette that finds every action by name. Two ways to use Canopy Start in a window. Grow into the keyboard. Both apps run the real git and GitHub CLI on your computer, with your own login. They share themes and settings in ~/.canopy , so you can use both. Start here Canopy Desktop The friendly one. For your first commit, and for every day after it. Next steps say, in plain words, where you are and give you a button for what comes next. Every term explained: hover (or press ? ) on staged, changed, ahead and behind to see what they mean. Asks before anything destructive, and says exactly what would be lost. Undo takes back the last commit, reset or checkout with ⌘Z . Shows the git command behind every action, so you learn git as you go. Get Canopy Desktop Everything, from the keyboard Canopy for the terminal The power tool. Every git and GitHub action one key away, and new power features land here first. Line-level staging, interactive rebase, fixups, cherry-picks, bisect and worktrees. Every repository at once in the Workspace tab, plus notifications, releases and inline PR reviews. Vim-style keys you can remap, custom commands on any key, and a command palette. Real git underneath: your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all just work. A single ~4 MB binary that starts instantly. Teach mode and next steps are still there when you want them. Tour the terminal app # both apps show what they ran. You press space on a line in a diff: $ git apply --cached --unidiff-zero --whitespace=nowarn - # stdin: a patch with just the selected lines # you undo a commit: $ git reset --soft 1f3a9c2 ← what that just ran Roadmap Where Canopy is growing. Built in the open, one phase at a time. Read the changelog → v0.1 The git dashboard v0.2 Remappable keys, tags & remotes, conflicts one by one v0.3 GitHub PRs, issues & CI · blame, bisect, worktrees v0.4 The Canopy wiki, the logo, and a roomier TUI v1.0 Notifications, releases, inline reviews, CI logs · Windows & winget v1.0.2 Canopy Desktop for macOS & Linux (preview) v1.0.3 Canopy Desktop on Windows 11 · no-compiler Homebrew v1.0.4 Start a new repository, HTTPS or SSH explained v1.0.5 Fix with AI: hand a failing CI run to Claude Code or Codex v1.0.6 Details and View in GitHub for failed runs v1.0.7 Your own themes and settings, shared by both apps v1.0.8 Desktop to start, the terminal for power: plain-language help in the app Next Notarized Mac builds · signed Windows installer Install Canopy Star on GitHub"}, {"url": "features.html", "title": "Canopy for the terminal", "section": "Product", "lede": "Canopy for the terminal: git and GitHub, everything from the keyboard. A tour of every tab, with real screenshots.", "headings": [{"id": "home", "text": "1 · Home"}, {"id": "changes", "text": "2 · Changes"}, {"id": "history", "text": "3 · History"}, {"id": "conflicts", "text": "Conflicts"}, {"id": "refs", "text": "4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees & submodules"}, {"id": "stash", "text": "5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog"}, {"id": "workspace", "text": "6 · Workspace"}, {"id": "undo", "text": "Undo"}, {"id": "bisect", "text": "Bisect"}, {"id": "palette", "text": "Command palette & teach mode"}], "text": "On this page Home Changes History Conflicts Branches & more Stash & Reflog Workspace Undo Bisect Palette & teach mode New to git? Start with Canopy Desktop : the same repository view in a window, with buttons, plain-language explanations and undo. Come back here when you want more power. It keeps teach mode, next steps and the wiki . 1 · Home Your repository at a glance: the branch and whether it's in sync, what's staged and changed, recent commits with a graph, commit activity, and your branches. Next steps reads the state of the repo and tells you, in plain words, what to do next and which key does it. canopy — Home 2 · Changes Everything you've changed, grouped as conflicts, changes, and staged. space stages or unstages a file, a does all of them, d discards (after telling you exactly what you'll lose). Press ↵ to step into the diff. There, space stages just the line under the cursor , v selects a range, and ↵ takes the whole hunk. s switches to a side-by-side view. canopy — Changes When you're ready, c opens the commit dialog, with a live length guide for the summary line. A amends the last commit instead. 3 · History The commit graph for your branch, with branch and tag labels. / filters by message, author or hash. On any commit: check it out, cherry-pick C , revert t , reset r (soft, mixed or hard), tag T , branch from it n , or fold staged changes into it F . canopy — History i opens a visual interactive rebase from the selected commit: reorder with J / K , and pick, squash, fixup, edit or drop each commit. L on a file shows its history (following renames) and B shows blame, where B again steps back to the version before that line changed. canopy — Blame Conflicts When a merge or rebase stops on conflicts, the header says so and Home tells you what to do. Open a conflicted file with ↵ and resolve it one conflict at a time: o keeps yours, t takes theirs, b keeps both. The file is written as you go and staged when nothing's left; u puts the markers back if you change your mind. Then C continues the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts 4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees & submodules One tab, five views: [ and ] switch between them. Branches: local and remote, with ahead/behind. Checkout, create, rename, delete, merge M , rebase r , set upstream. Tags: create ( name: message makes an annotated tag), push, delete here or everywhere. Remotes: add, rename, change URL, fetch one, remove. Worktrees: check out another branch in its own folder, open it in Canopy, remove or prune. Submodules: see which are in sync, update one or all, open one. canopy — Branches 5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog Stash shelves your uncommitted work ( S from anywhere) so you can switch tasks; apply, pop or drop it later. Reflog lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits you \"lost\" to a reset. Reset back to any entry from there. 6 · Workspace Point Canopy at your code folders ( workspace_dirs in the config, or canopy -w ~/code ) and see every repository at once: branch, uncommitted changes, conflicts, and whether it's ahead or behind. ↵ opens one; F fetches them all. canopy — Workspace Undo z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, using git's reflog. Canopy tells you what it's about to do first: After a commit : the commit is undone and its changes come back staged . After a checkout : you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase : the branch moves back, and your uncommitted changes are kept. Bisect Something broke, but you don't know when. In History, select a commit where it still worked and press b . Git checks out a commit halfway between; test it, press b , and answer good or bad. The header counts down the steps left, and when git finds the culprit, Canopy selects it in History. Command palette & teach mode : or ⌃P opens the palette: type a few letters of any action and press ↵ . ! runs any git command you like. With teach mode on ( T toggles it), the footer shows the exact git command behind each action you take. can"}, {"url": "github.html", "title": "GitHub", "section": "Product", "lede": "Pull requests, issues and CI runs in your terminal, through the GitHub CLI.", "headings": [{"id": "setup", "text": "Setup"}, {"id": "prs", "text": "8 · Pull requests"}, {"id": "issues", "text": "9 · Issues"}, {"id": "actions", "text": "0 · Actions"}, {"id": "home", "text": "On Home"}, {"id": "privacy", "text": "What Canopy sends"}, {"id": "fix-with-ai", "text": "Fix with AI"}], "text": "On this page Setup Pull requests Line comments Issues Notifications Actions CI logs Releases Fix with AI On Home What Canopy sends Setup GitHub features need gh , logged in. Canopy never installs anything itself; if gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs show exactly what to run. $ brew install gh $ gh auth login That's it. Open Canopy in any repository whose remote is on github.com. 8 · Pull requests Open pull requests with their checks ( ✓ 2/2 , ✗ 1 failed ), review state, author, and age. f cycles between open, yours, review requested, and all. The side panel shows the description, every check, reviews, and comments; D adds the diff, with review comments shown under the lines they're about. canopy — Pull requests ▄ ▀▀▀▀ ▄ c a n o p y v1.0.5 acme-app main ↑1 ▀ ▀▀ ▀ 1 Home 2 Changes 2 3 Log 4 Refs 5 Stash 6 Repos 7 Reflog 8 PRs 2 9 Issues 0 CI ╭ Pull requests · o/r · open · 2 ─────────────────────────╮ ╭ #12 Add CSV parser ────────────────────────────────────╮ │ │ │ │ │ #12 ada ✗ 1 failed approved 22h │ │ #12 Add CSV parser │ │ #11 bob 1d │ │ ada wants to merge feature/parser into main │ │ │ │ open · +317 -15 · updated 21h │ │ │ │ review: ✓ approved │ │ │ │ ✓ no conflicts with the base branch │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ## Summary │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ Parses CSV. │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ── bob approved · 21h │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ── bob commented · 21h │ │ │ │ Nice! │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ╰─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ $ teach mode: the git command behind each action appears here (T to hide) space checkout n new r review M merge D diff o browser f filter : palette ? help space Check out the pull request's branch n Open a pull request for the current branch (push it first with P ) r Review: approve, comment, or request changes C Comment on the pull request D Show the diff, with review comments inline ↵ then C In the diff: comment on the line under the cursor M Merge: merge commit, squash, or rebase (warns when checks fail) X Close o Open in your browser Comment on a line Press D to show the diff, ↵ to move into it, and put the cursor on the line you mean. Press C , write the comment, and press ⌃S . It shows up on GitHub as a review comment on that line. Comments on removed lines go on the old side of the diff. Existing comments appear right under their lines, with the author and when they wrote it. 9 · Issues Open, assigned-to-you, or all issues, with labels and comment counts. Read the full discussion in the side panel, then n to file one, C to comment, X to close (or reopen a closed one). Notifications Press ] on Issues to switch to your GitHub inbox: review requests, mentions, assignments and CI results, newest first, with unread ones marked ● . f switches between this repository and everywhere, and u between unread and all. ↵ / o Open it in your browser m Mark read M Mark all read (this repository, or everywhere) canopy — Issues ▄ ▀▀▀▀ ▄ c a n o p y v1.0.5 acme-app main ↑1 ▀ ▀▀ ▀ 1 Home 2 Changes 2 3 Log 4 Refs 5 Stash 6 Repos 7 Reflog 8 PRs 2 9 Issues 0 CI ╭ Issues · Notifications o/r · open · 1 ─────────────────╮ ╭ #7 Crash on empty repo ────────────────────────────────╮ │ │ │ │ │ #7 Crash on e carol 1 comment 5d │ │ #7 Crash on empty repo │ │ │ │ open · opened by carol · updated 5d · 1 comment(s) │ │ │ │ labels: bug │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ Opening Canopy in a repo with no commits crashes │ │ │ │ when you press 3. │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ── ada commented · 6d │ │ │ │ On it │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ ╰─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ $ teach mode: the git command behind each action appears here (T to hide) ] switch view n new C comment X close o browser f filter : palette q quit ? help 0 · Actions Workflow runs for your branch ( f for all branches), refreshing every few seconds while any "}, {"url": "desktop.html", "title": "Canopy Desktop", "section": "Product", "lede": "Canopy Desktop: the friendly way to start with git and GitHub, on Mac, Windows and Linux. Release preview.", "headings": [{"id": "preview", "text": "A release preview"}, {"id": "install", "text": "Install"}, {"id": "learning", "text": "Made for learning"}, {"id": "home", "text": "Home"}, {"id": "changes", "text": "Changes"}, {"id": "history", "text": "History"}, {"id": "github", "text": "GitHub"}, {"id": "fix-with-ai", "text": "Fix with AI"}, {"id": "new-repo", "text": "Start a new repository"}, {"id": "settings", "text": "Settings & updates"}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows"}, {"id": "keys", "text": "Keys"}, {"id": "more", "text": "Ready for more? Canopy for the terminal"}], "text": "On this page Release preview Install Made for learning Home Changes History GitHub Fix with AI New repository Settings & updates Windows Keys A release preview Not notarized by Apple yet. Canopy Desktop is a release preview: the Mac app isn't signed with an Apple Developer ID and notarized yet (that's coming). The first time you open it, macOS asks you to confirm: open System Settings → Privacy & Security and click Open Anyway , or run xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/canopy.app . Linux builds don't need any of this. The desktop app runs the real git and GitHub CLI on your machine, exactly like the terminal app. It never sends your code anywhere; the only thing it asks the internet for is the latest version number, once a day (you can turn that off). Install macOS, with Homebrew That puts canopy.app in your Applications folder. Update it later with brew upgrade --cask canopy-desktop . Or download the .dmg for Apple silicon or Intel from the latest release . Linux Each release has a .deb (Debian, Ubuntu and friends) and an AppImage (any distribution) for x86_64: From source On Linux this needs the WebKitGTK development packages ( libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev ). Canopy Desktop needs git ; the GitHub pages also use the GitHub CLI . Already using the terminal app? Press ⌥D (or search \"desktop\" in the palette) and it hands you the install command. Made for learning Next steps with buttons. Home says in plain words where your repository stands (changes to commit, commits to push, a merge in progress) and gives you a button for each step, including Connect to GitHub… when a repository isn't backed up anywhere yet. Every term explained. Hover over Staged, Changed, Conflicts, Stashes, Branches or Remotes (or click its ? ) to see what it means. The ↑ and ↓ counts explain themselves too. A short introduction the first time: change, stage, commit, push, with links to Git in ten minutes . Dismiss it, and bring it back from Settings → General. A safety net. Canopy asks before anything that throws work away and says exactly what would be lost. After a commit, checkout, merge or reset, it reminds you that ⌘Z undoes it. Learn git as you go. With Show git commands on (the default), every action shows the command Canopy ran. Home Where your branch stands, what's staged and changed, and next steps with a button for each: commit, push, pull, resolve. If this branch has a pull request, its checks and review are right there. Changes Staged, changed and conflicted files on the left; the diff on the right. Stage a whole file, a hunk, or just the lines you click (shift-click for a range). Write the message below and commit with ⌘↵ . Conflicts get keep-mine / take-theirs buttons and a banner to continue or abort the merge. History The commit graph, drawn properly: merges, branches and tags. Search messages (or @name for an author), see any commit's full diff, and check out, branch, tag, cherry-pick, revert or reset from there. Branches and Stash have pages of their own, and Undo ( ⌘Z ) reverses the last commit, reset or checkout. GitHub Pull requests with their checks, reviews and comments: check out, review, merge, comment, or open a new one from your branch. Issues, and Actions runs with each job's steps and the end of the failed log. It all goes through your own gh login. Fix with AI When CI fails on your latest commit, Home says so: Canopy found an error in your latest commit , with what failed. Details shows exactly what went wrong in a popup (copy it, open it in an editor, or View in GitHub ), and Fix with Claude Code (or Codex, or your own command, set in Settings → AI assistant) writes a prompt from the failure and opens the assistant in a new terminal window to fix it. The same button is on every failed run in Actions. How it works . Start a new repository Choose Start a new one… on the welcome screen, or open a folder that isn't a repository yet. Canopy sets it up (branch, .gitignore , README, license, first commit) and can connect it to "}, {"url": "changelog.html", "title": "Changelog", "section": "Product", "lede": "What's new in each Canopy release.", "headings": [{"id": "next", "text": "What's next"}], "text": "On this page v1.0.8 v1.0.7 v1.0.6 v1.0.5 v1.0.4 v1.0.3 v1.0.2 v1.0.1 v1.0.0 v0.4.0 v0.3.0 v0.2.0 v0.1.0 What's next v1.0.8 Latest September 2026 One Canopy, two ways to use it. Canopy Desktop is where to start. Canopy for the terminal does everything, from the keyboard. Nothing is renamed: the canopy command, Homebrew and winget packages stay the same. Desktop app: every term explained . Hover over (or click ? on) Staged, Changed, Conflicts, Stashes, Branches and Remotes on Home, and over the ↑/↓ counts, to see what they mean. Desktop app: a short New to git? card on Home (change, stage, commit, push) with links to the wiki. Dismiss it; Settings → General brings it back. Desktop app: a button on every next step . Connect to GitHub… when a repository has no remote (create it on GitHub, or connect an existing one over HTTPS or SSH), and Open History during a bisect. Desktop app: after a commit, checkout, merge, cherry-pick, revert or reset, the message reminds you that ⌘Z undoes it. Desktop app: Settings → General shows whether the terminal app is installed, and the command to install it. The website leads with the desktop app. The terminal app's tour is now Canopy for the terminal , and Get started has a path for each app. v1.0.7 September 2026 Your own themes , shared between the terminal and desktop apps. Themes are JSON files in ~/.canopy/themes/ . The terminal app picks them up for theme , -t and ⌃T ; canopy --list-themes lists them and canopy --export-theme nord gives you one to start from. Desktop app: Settings has a new Appearance page with every theme (catppuccin, gruvbox and nord are new there) and a theme editor : pick colors and see them live, with a preview of the terminal app. Save it and the terminal app can use it straight away. Themes can be imported, exported, or opened in your editor as JSON. Move settings between the apps : the theme, text editor, AI assistant and the show-commands setting. canopy --export-settings / --import-settings FILE in the terminal; Import from terminal app , Send to terminal app , Export and Import in the desktop app. Choose your text editor : editor = \"code --wait\" in the terminal app's config; in the desktop app, Settings lists the editors it finds (VS Code, Cursor, Zed, Sublime Text, Neovim, Vim, Helix, nano, Emacs) or takes any command. Terminal editors open in a new terminal window. Desktop app: Open in editor on the Changes page (and on conflicted files); Details on a failed run opens in your chosen editor. Canopy's folder is now ~/.canopy ( CANOPY_HOME to change it). Your settings from ~/.config/canopy are copied over the first time 1.0.7 runs. v1.0.6 September 2026 Details for a failed run: the full write-up of what went wrong. Desktop: a popup with Copy, Open in editor and View in GitHub, on the Home banner and on failed runs. Terminal: e on a failed run opens it in your editor. View in GitHub buttons across the desktop app, with the GitHub mark; the terminal app's o hint says so too. Fix with … buttons show the assistant's mark: Claude for Claude Code, OpenAI for Codex. Fixed: color codes that GitHub writes out as text ( ^[[1m ) no longer end up in the prompt. Fixed: the desktop AI assistant setting showed \"Custom command\" for settings saved before 1.0.5. v1.0.5 September 2026 Fix with AI : when CI fails, hand it to Claude Code or Codex. Canopy writes a prompt from the failure (the failed job and step, GitHub's summary, including why a workflow file is invalid, and the cleaned-up log) into .git/canopy/fix-ci.md , then opens your assistant in a new terminal tab or window, already working on it. Terminal app: A on a failed run in Actions. Home's next steps point to it when checks fail. Configure with ai and ai_open . Desktop app: a banner on Home when your latest commit fails, and Fix with … on failed runs; choose the assistant in Settings. Works with Claude Code, Codex (whichever is installed) or any command you set. v1.0.4 September 2026 Start a new repository , in the termi"}, {"url": "get-started.html", "title": "Get started", "section": "Docs", "lede": "Install Canopy: the desktop app to start with, or the terminal app with Homebrew, winget, a script or Cargo.", "headings": [{"id": "brew", "text": "Install with Homebrew"}, {"id": "script", "text": "Install without Homebrew"}, {"id": "cargo", "text": "Install with Cargo"}, {"id": "source", "text": "Run from a clone of the source"}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows"}, {"id": "first", "text": "First run"}, {"id": "config", "text": "Configuration"}, {"id": "themes", "text": "Themes"}, {"id": "update", "text": "Updating"}], "text": "On this page Homebrew Install script With Cargo From source Windows First run Configuration Themes Updating Recommended if you're new Canopy Desktop A friendly app for macOS, Windows and Linux, with buttons, explanations and undo. brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop on a Mac, winget install shankars.canopy-desktop on Windows. Everything, from the keyboard Canopy for the terminal The power tool: every git and GitHub action one key away. Install it below with Homebrew, winget, a script or Cargo, then run canopy in any repository. You can use both: they share themes and settings in ~/.canopy . The rest of this page is about the terminal app; the desktop app has its own page . Install with Homebrew The easiest way on a Mac, and on Linux if you use Homebrew there. It installs a prebuilt binary, so nothing is compiled: Then run canopy inside any git repository. Install without Homebrew On macOS or Linux (x86_64), this script downloads the latest release, checks its SHA-256, and puts canopy in ~/.local/bin . It doesn't need sudo. Read it first if you like: scripts/install.sh . A specific version: … | sh -s -- v1.0.0 . Somewhere else: CANOPY_INSTALL_DIR=~/bin . To remove it: … | sh -s -- --uninstall (your config stays). Install with Cargo If you have Rust installed ( rustup.rs ), Cargo can build and install Canopy straight from GitHub. It lands in ~/.cargo/bin , which rustup already put on your PATH : Why canopy-git-tui ? That's the package name (the name canopy was already taken on crates.io). The command it installs is still canopy . Run from a clone of the source To try the latest code, or hack on it: cargo run builds and starts Canopy in one step; the first build takes a minute or two, later ones are quick. Without a path after -- , Canopy opens the repository you're in. Since you're in the Canopy clone itself, that's a handy way to try it on a real history. Running the tests: cargo test --workspace . Windows Canopy runs in Windows Terminal (recommended) and in PowerShell. Homebrew is for macOS and Linux, so on Windows install it one of these ways. With winget winget installs Canopy (x64 or Arm), puts canopy on your PATH , and installs git too if you don't have it: Update later with winget upgrade shankars.canopy . For the GitHub tabs, also install and log in to the GitHub CLI: Open a new terminal, then run canopy inside any repository. If winget doesn't find the package yet (new versions take a few days to be approved), use the install script below. 1. Or: install git (and optionally the GitHub CLI) 2. Install Canopy The install script downloads the latest release, checks it, installs it to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\canopy , and adds that folder to your PATH . It doesn't need admin rights ( read it ): Open a new terminal, then run canopy . Or do it by hand: 2a. Download Canopy Every release includes canopy-<version>-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip , and from 1.0.1 canopy-<version>-aarch64-pc-windows-msvc.zip for Windows on Arm. Unzip it and put canopy.exe somewhere on your PATH , for example: Open a new terminal window so the new PATH takes effect, then run canopy inside any repository. 2b. Or build it with Cargo On Windows: the config file is %USERPROFILE%\\.canopy\\config.toml . \"alt\" shortcuts use the Alt key. Custom commands run with cmd /C , e opens files in the editor set in VISUAL or EDITOR (for example code --wait ), and teach mode shows commands quoted for PowerShell. First run The first time, Canopy shows a short welcome with the keys that matter and writes a starter config. From then on: 1 – 0 switch tabs; the footer always lists the keys that work right now. ? shows every key for the current screen; : searches every action by name. q quits. ⌃Q , ⌥Q and ⌃C quit from anywhere, even with a dialog open. Configuration The config file lives at ~/.canopy/config.toml ( canopy --config-path prints it; canopy --example-config prints a template). Themes Five built in: canopy (forest greens), catppuccin , gruvbox , no"}, {"url": "keys.html", "title": "Keys", "section": "Docs", "lede": "Every Canopy key, generated from the app itself.", "headings": [{"id": "k-global", "text": "Everywhere"}, {"id": "k-status", "text": "Changes"}, {"id": "k-diff", "text": "Diff panel (after ↵)"}, {"id": "k-conflict", "text": "Conflict panel"}, {"id": "k-log", "text": "History"}, {"id": "k-branches", "text": "Branches"}, {"id": "k-tags", "text": "Tags"}, {"id": "k-remotes", "text": "Remotes"}, {"id": "k-worktrees", "text": "Worktrees"}, {"id": "k-submodules", "text": "Submodules"}, {"id": "k-stash", "text": "Stash"}, {"id": "k-workspace", "text": "Workspace"}, {"id": "k-reflog", "text": "Reflog"}, {"id": "k-pulls", "text": "Pull requests"}, {"id": "k-issues", "text": "Issues"}, {"id": "k-runs", "text": "Actions"}], "text": "On this page Everywhere Changes Diff panel Conflict panel History Branches Tags Remotes Worktrees Submodules Stash Workspace Reflog Pull requests Issues Actions Keys are shown the Mac way: ⌃ is Control, ⌥ is Option (Alt on other keyboards), ⇧ is Shift. Option shortcuts work in any terminal, including macOS Terminal's default setup where Option types special characters. ⌃Q and ⌥Q quit from anywhere, even inside a dialog. In Canopy itself, Linux shows ctrl-q / alt-q instead; set mac_key_symbols in the config to choose. Everywhere ? ⌥H f1 help help : ⌃P ⌃K command palette palette q ⌃Q ⌥Q ⌃C quit quit 1 ⌥1 go to home goto_home 2 ⌥2 go to changes goto_changes 3 ⌥3 go to history goto_history 4 ⌥4 go to branches goto_branches 5 ⌥5 go to stash goto_stash 6 ⌥6 go to workspace goto_workspace 7 ⌥7 go to reflog goto_reflog 8 ⌥8 go to pull requests goto_pull requests 9 ⌥9 go to issues goto_issues 0 ⌥0 go to actions goto_actions tab ⌥→ ⌃→ next tab next_screen ⇧tab ⌥← ⌃← previous tab prev_screen ⌃R ⌥R refresh refresh ⌃L redraw the screen redraw c ⌥C ⌃S commit commit f ⌥F fetch fetch p ⌥P pull pull P ⌥⇧P push push z ⌥Z undo last action undo S ⌥S stash changes stash_push b bisect: find the commit that broke something bisect ! ⌥G run git command raw_git T toggle teach mode toggle_teach ⌃T ⌥T cycle theme cycle_theme k ↑ up up j ↓ down down ⌃U pageup page up page_up ⌃D pagedown page down page_down g home top top G end bottom bottom esc back back Changes space stage/unstage toggle_stage a stage/unstage all stage_all d discard changes discard ↵ l open enter A amend last commit amend e open in editor open_editor i add to .gitignore ignore o resolve: keep ours take_ours t resolve: take theirs take_theirs C continue merge/rebase continue_op X abort merge/rebase abort_op L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame Diff panel (after ↵) space stage/unstage line(s) stage_line ↵ stage/unstage hunk stage_hunk v select range range_select n ] next hunk next_hunk N [ previous hunk prev_hunk s toggle split view toggle_side_by_side h ← back back L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame C comment on this line of a pull request line_comment Conflict panel o keep ours for this conflict keep_ours t take theirs for this conflict keep_theirs b keep both (ours, then theirs) keep_both j ↓ n next conflict next_conflict k ↑ N previous conflict prev_conflict u restore conflict markers restore_conflict e open in editor open_editor h ← back back History ↵ l open enter space checkout commit checkout_commit C cherry-pick cherry_pick t revert commit revert r reset to commit reset_menu i interactive rebase from here rebase_interactive n new branch here branch_from_commit T tag commit tag_commit F fixup staged into commit fixup_commit y copy hash copy_hash / search search Branches space ↵ checkout checkout n new branch new_branch d delete branch delete_branch M merge into current merge r rebase current onto rebase R rename branch rename_branch u set upstream set_upstream / search search ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Tags space ↵ checkout tag checkout_tag n new tag at HEAD new_tag d delete tag delete_tag P push tag push_tag ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view / search search Remotes f fetch this remote fetch_remote n add remote add_remote d remove remote remove_remote e change remote URL edit_remote_url R rename remote rename_remote ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Worktrees ↵ space open worktree in Canopy open_worktree n new worktree for a branch new_worktree d remove worktree remove_worktree x prune missing worktrees prune_worktrees ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Submodules ↵ space open submodule in Canopy open_submodule u update "}];
